Hi Nate,

 'which suggests that you have many RTs near 0 in your data. Is that the case?'

Not the case at all. If fact, this was done using a pretty restrictive cut-off for lower bound of decision (.3 s). I recently thought that this rather aggressive outlier removal strategy might be at fault here, so I used a different, conservative method of outlier removal on individual level and run the model again. The results are, well, weird...

                  mean se_mean     sd      2.5%       25%       50%       75%     97.5% n_eff Rhat
mu_d              1.09    0.01   0.76      0.06      0.49      0.96      1.56      2.86  5000 1.00
mu_A              1.01    0.04   0.69      0.05      0.46      0.91      1.46      2.58   320 1.01
mu_v[1,1]         3.24    0.00   0.10      3.04      3.17      3.24      3.31      3.45   816 1.00
mu_v[1,2]         2.41    0.00   0.12      2.16      2.33      2.41      2.49      2.66  1054 1.00
mu_v[2,1]         2.64    0.00   0.09      2.46      2.58      2.64      2.70      2.81   629 1.00
mu_v[2,2]         1.79    0.00   0.12      1.57      1.72      1.79      1.87      2.02  1213 1.00
mu_v[3,1]         2.33    0.00   0.08      2.17      2.28      2.33      2.39      2.50   650 1.00
mu_v[3,2]         1.64    0.00   0.09      1.47      1.58      1.64      1.70      1.82   689 1.00
mu_tau            0.70    0.01   0.41      0.06      0.38      0.66      0.96      1.58  5000 1.00
sigma_d         443.90    0.53  14.91    414.70    433.79    443.58    453.89    473.21   778 1.00
sigma_A           1.17    0.07   1.06      0.07      0.40      0.86      1.61      4.06   213 1.01

This time the non-decision time is impossibly long (even though less short reaction times were removed). If you look at the individual level you can see that for some participants the estimated non-decision time now is longer than average RT...

Although the number of divergent transitions was much smaller this time (74), which I think is a step in the right direction...? In this case however, restricting minimum non-decision time would not help. Do you have any ideas on how to proceed?

Best,
Wojciech

