2. In order to force  the non-decision times to have realistic values I constrained the tau priors even further not allowing them to go below 100 ms:

mu_tau ~ normal(.25,.15)T[0.1,];

Just in case I increased the adapt delta to .985.

Unfortunately, this model had 4986 divergent transitions (out of 5000 samples) and identical estimates of non-dec times were estimated for each subject (0.11; so basically almost the allowed minimum).

It seems that the model consistently estimates non-dec times to be impossibly short. This is clearly biologically implausible, as simple reaction times (where no decision is involved) take longer that 100ms.

I am interested in your point about how participants clear preferences among each pair might contribute to difficulties in identifying separate effects of different parameters, because it is not quite clear to me. I'll post individual pair plots in a few hours. Sorry for the delay.

Best,
Wojciech

